# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r2 and locke-labour.logic.r2.3 have "removes → mixed" and "mixed ∧ labour_his → property"; locke-labour.logic.r2.2 has "removes → mixed"; they differ when only "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his", "he removes a thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in" and "he hath mixed his labour with it and joined to it something that is his own" are true, which the second allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r2 and locke-labour.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his", "he removes a thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in" and "he hath mixed his labour with it and joined to it something that is his own" are true and "he makes it his property" is false. Is S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property.", true then?
locke-labour.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r2 and locke-labour.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; locke-labour.logic.r2.2 has "excludes → property"; they differ when only "the labour annexed to it excludes the common right of other men, so that no man but he can have a right to it" is true, which the first allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 3 ways: locke-labour.logic.r2 has "removes ∧ enough → excludes"; locke-labour.logic.r2.2 has "removes ∧ enough → property"; they differ when only "he removes a thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in", "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others" and "the labour annexed to it excludes the common right of other men, so that no man but he can have a right to it" are true, which the first allows; locke-labour.logic.r2.3 has "removes → property"
locke-labour.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: locke-labour.logic.r2 puts it at S4, "It being by him removed from the common state nature hath placed it in, it hath by this labour something annexed to it, that excludes the common right of other men: for this labour being the unquestionable property of the labourer, no man but he can have a right to what that is once joined to, at least where there is enough, and as good, left in common for others."; locke-labour.logic.r2.2 and locke-labour.logic.r2.3 at S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property."
locke-labour.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r2.2 and locke-labour.logic.r2.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S4, "It being by him removed from the common state nature hath placed it in, it hath by this labour something annexed to it, that excludes the common right of other men: for this labour being the unquestionable property of the labourer, no man but he can have a right to what that is once joined to, at least where there is enough, and as good, left in common for others.", rather than S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property."?
locke-labour.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: locke-labour.logic.r2.2 takes for granted "if the labour annexed to it excludes the common right of other men, so that no man but he can have a right to it, then he makes it his property"; locke-labour.logic.r2 and locke-labour.logic.r2.3 do not
locke-labour.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r2.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the labour annexed to it excludes the common right of other men, so that no man but he can have a right to it, then he makes it his property"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
